---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site-config';
import { SITES, BRAND } from '../../lib/sites';
import { articleJsonLd, breadcrumbJsonLd } from '../../lib/seo';

const canonical = `${SITES.data}/explain/how-specs-are-measured/`;
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Explainers', url: '/explain/' },
  { name: 'How specifications are measured', url: '/explain/how-specs-are-measured/' },
];

const jsonLd = [
  articleJsonLd({
    id: canonical,
    headline: 'How charcoal specifications are measured',
    description:
      'The method families behind charcoal specification values — ASTM D1762, ASTM D3172 and the ISO 181xx series for fuel charcoal, and why GB/T 12496 applies to activated carbon, not fuel.',
    datePublished: '2026-10-07',
    dateModified: '2026-10-07',
  }),
  breadcrumbJsonLd([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${SITES.data}/` },
    { name: 'Explainers', url: `${SITES.data}/explain/` },
    { name: 'How specifications are measured', url: canonical },
  ]),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  site={site}
  title="How Charcoal Specs Are Measured"
  description="The method families behind charcoal specification values: ASTM D1762, ASTM D3172 and the ISO 181xx series for fuel charcoal, and why GB/T 12496 applies to activated carbon, not fuel."
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  ogType="article"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="page-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">Explainer</p>
      <h1>How charcoal specifications are measured</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A specification value is only meaningful when you know the method family that
        produced it. This page explains the standard families most often cited on
        charcoal datasheets, what each one applies to, and where suppliers commonly
        confuse them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="callout-title">Methodology, not test data</span>
      <p>
        This page describes public standard method families in general terms. It
        contains no {BRAND} test result and no supplier-specific value.
      </p>
    </div>

    <article class="prose">
      <h2>The two method families behind fuel-charcoal numbers</h2>
      <p>
        Most fuel-charcoal specification values come from one of two method families.
        The first is <strong>ASTM D1762</strong>, the standard chemical-analysis method
        for wood charcoal, which reports moisture, volatile matter, ash and (by
        difference) fixed carbon. The second is the <strong>ASTM D3172</strong> proximate-analysis
        family for coal and coke, where the individual determinations are split across
        companion methods — D3173 for moisture, D3174 for ash and D3175 for volatile
        matter — with fixed carbon again calculated by difference.
      </p>
      <p>
        A third, more modern family is the <strong>ISO 181xx series for solid biofuels</strong>,
        which mirrors the same proximate analysis (ISO 18134 for moisture, ISO 18122 for
        ash, ISO 18123 for volatile matter). Suppliers serving markets where solid-biofuel
        norms are recognised increasingly cite ISO rather than ASTM.
      </p>

      <h2>Where GB/T 12496 fits — and where it does not</h2>
      <p>
        A recurring confusion on Chinese-sourced datasheets is the citation of
        <strong>GB/T 12496</strong>, the Chinese national standard family for
        <em>wooden activated carbon</em> test methods. This family covers parameters such as
        iodine number and methylene blue adsorption — properties of activated carbon used
        in filtration — and includes an ash determination (GB/T 12496.3). It applies to the
        activated-carbon product family, <strong>not to fuel charcoal</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a fuel-charcoal supplier quotes "GB/T 12496" alongside ash or moisture, treat it
        as a signal to ask a follow-up question: the value may genuinely have been measured
        that way, or the supplier may have copied a method reference from an activated-carbon
        line. The two product families are not interchangeable, and their ash specifications
        describe different things.
      </p>

      <h2>What "measured" versus "calculated" means</h2>
      <p>
        Of the four proximate parameters, three are measured directly — moisture, volatile
        matter and ash — and one, <strong>fixed carbon</strong>, is calculated as
        <em>100% − moisture − volatile matter − ash</em>. This matters because a calculated
        value inherits the uncertainty of its three inputs: an error in any one of them moves
        the fixed-carbon figure. A datasheet whose four proximate numbers do not reconcile is
        internally inconsistent.
      </p>

      <h2>Which method matters for comparability</h2>
      <p>
        Different method families heat samples at different temperatures and report on
        different bases, so a value is only comparable against another value obtained by the
        same method, on the same basis. Before comparing two suppliers, confirm the method
        and the reporting basis — the subject of
        <a href="/explain/test-basis-matters/">why the test basis matters</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Where to go next</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/specifications/">All specification methodology pages</a> — per-metric detail</li>
        <li><a href="/explain/how-to-read-a-spec-sheet/">How to read a spec sheet</a></li>
        <li><a href="/explain/how-to-compare/">How to compare two suppliers</a></li>
        <li><a href="/products/">Product database</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${SITES.testing}/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Testing sub-site</a> — laboratory-side method detail</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
